doc.para:
How to Write Functions
　Functions can be written in the following format:

	function function_name ( argument_list )
	{
		function content
	}


　First, to define a function, write function. After that, write the name of the function. The function will become usable with the name written here.
　In the function content, you can describe statements or blocks that you want to execute inside the function.


	function test(a) { System.inform(a); }
	function func(a, b, c, d)
	{
		test(a);
		test(b);
		test(c);
		test(d);
		return a+b-c*d;
	}


When using the instanceof operator on a function with "Function", it evaluates to true (in the above example, func instanceof "Function" is true).

Function Calls
　A function call is written using the ( ) operator as follows:

	function_name( arguments )

	If there are no arguments, it becomes function_name( ). For arguments, specify expressions, and if there are multiple, separate them with commas.

	func();
	func(1+2, 1-2);
	func(func2());


	Also, if an expression is omitted and left blank, it is considered as if void was specified for that part.

	func(,1); // Treated as if void, 1 were specified
	func(,); // Treated as if void, void were specified

Argument List
　In the argument list, write the names of variables to receive the passed arguments. Arguments passed during a function call are handed over to the variables sequentially from left to right.

	function test(a, b, c)
	{
		// At this point, the variables a, b, and c are available,
		// and the 3 passed arguments are assigned to them.
		// If called as test(1, 2, 3), a contains 1,
		// b contains 2, and c contains 3.
	}


　These arguments are local variables and cannot be accessed once execution leaves the function.

　In TJS2, it is not an error if the number of arguments provided during a function call is fewer or more than the declared number of arguments (as an exception, functions not written in TJS2—such as those whose body is written in C++—may throw an error). If more arguments are given than declared, the excess ones are ignored; if fewer, void is passed to the missing variables.

	function test(a, b)
	{
		// If called as test(1), a gets 1 and b gets void.
		// If called as test(1,2,3), a gets 1, b gets 2, and 3 is ignored.
	}


　Individual arguments in the argument list can have a default value (default argument) specified following =. This means that when void is specified, or when fewer arguments are provided than declared, the specified value is automatically used.

	function test(a = -1, b = 1)
	{
		// If...
		// Called as test(), a=-1, b=1
		// Called as test(5), a=5, b=1
		// Called as test(void,void), a=-1, b=1
		// Called as test(,4), a=-1, b=4
	}


　Functions that do not need to receive arguments can omit the argument list.

	function test
	{
		// Function that accepts no arguments
	}

Omitting Arguments
　When calling a function, if you write only ... as the argument, the arguments passed to the function containing the call expression are passed directly to the target function. Even if the contents of the argument variables have been modified, or if fewer arguments were received, the original content and correct number of arguments will be passed.

	function test()
	{
		test2(...);
		// The arguments passed to test are passed to test2
		// with their original contents and count as when passed
	}

Converting Arguments to an Array
　You can receive arguments as an array. To receive arguments as an array, declare the function by appending '*' to the argument name.

	function func(args*)
	{
		// args becomes an array containing the arguments passed to this function as elements.
		// For example, if called as func(1, 2, 3, 4),
		// args contains 4 elements, holding numeric values 1 2 3 4
		// in that order from the beginning.
	}


　You can also receive arguments from a middle position to the end all as an array. If the required number of arguments is not met, the array will be empty.

	function func(x, args*)
	{
		// For example, if called as func(1, 2, 3, 4),
		// x gets 1. Also args contains 3 elements,
		// holding numeric values 2 3 4 in order from the beginning.
		// If called as func(1), since the number of arguments is insufficient,
		// args becomes an array with no elements.
	}

　This is convenient when you want to handle variable-length arguments.

　It is also possible not to specify an argument name. In this case, it can be used as an unnamed * during array expansion into arguments described later.

Expanding an Array into Arguments
　An array can be expanded and passed as arguments to a function. In this case, similar to converting arguments into an array, call the function with '*' appended to the expression you want to expand. The expression to expand with '*' must represent an array.

	var args = [1, 2, 3, 4];
	func(args*);
	// Since args contains the 4 elements 1 2 3 4,
	// this has the same meaning as func(1, 2, 3, 4);.
	func(0, args*, 5);
	// It can also be inserted in the middle of arguments like this.
	// In this case, it has the same meaning as
	// func(0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5);.


　If you specify simply * as an argument without specifying an expression, the arguments represented by the unnamed '*' written at the declaration of the function containing the call expression can be passed directly to the target.

	function func(func_array, *)
	{
		// Call each function specified in func_array,
		// passing its index in func_array followed by
		// the 2nd and subsequent arguments passed to func
		// as they are
		for(var i = 0; i < func_array.count; i++)
			func_array[i](i, *);
	}


　Function calling processes using an unnamed * can be processed more efficiently than using a named array.

	function func(ar*) { func2(ar*); }
	function func(*) { func2(*); }
	// The above two have the exact same meaning, but the lower example is more efficient


　An unnamed * has the same effect as "omitting arguments". The following two function declarations have the same meaning.

	function func() { func2(...); }
	function func(*) { func2(*); }
